WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.970
- [Narrator] Birria may
be new to some of you

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:04.340
but this dish has been around for decades.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.320
The juicy, slow cooked
meat stew is often served

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.320
for special occasions
and you'll soon see why.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.720
It's truly a melt in your mouth experience

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.400
that honestly deserves its own holiday.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.040
Today we celebrate its evolution
by not only showing you how

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.480
to make delicious birria,

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.409
but also how to serve it taco style.

00:00:23.409 --> 00:00:26.159
(relaxing music)

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.470
Birria originated in the
Mexican state of Jalisco,

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.910
but it's believed to
have grown in popularity

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.611
after Don Guadalupe Zarate
set up a birria stand

00:00:35.611 --> 00:00:38.240
in Tijuana in the 1950s,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.740
Birria is traditionally
made with goat meat,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.360
but reportedly Zarate eventually began

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.573
to prepare it with beef.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.270
We tested a variety of meats,

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.250
goat, beef, lamb, and pork.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.020
While we loved the classic goat,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.690
we decided to use beef for our recipe.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.870
It's more widely available
but still packed with flavor.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.500
Now that we've picked our protein,

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.040
let's talk cuts.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.580
We want fatty, meaty cuts.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.330
In this recipe we're using a
mix of boneless beef chuck roll

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.380
and bone-in beef short ribs.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.300
Bones are packed with collagen and flavor,

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:12.960
adding richness to our consome

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.639
that will have your lips
sticking together in a good way.

00:01:15.639 --> 00:01:18.300
Consome is a savory brothy sauce.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.664
It's what we'll be dipping
our tacos in later.

00:01:20.664 --> 00:01:22.360
Before we get to cooking,

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.040
we want to season our meat with salt.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.710
Don't skimp on the salt here, people.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.080
We want to coat our meat completely.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.150
Then we'll chill our
seasoned meat in the fridge

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.280
for at least four hours.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.920
Seasoning our meat before
we sear it is going

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.080
to give us that crispy brown crust

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.670
and lock in more flavor.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.100
All right, let's move on to our chilies.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.640
Everyone uses their own
combo of dried chilies.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.007
Today we're using or Morita,
Guajillo, and Ancho chilies.

00:01:47.007 --> 00:01:50.060
Morita chilies for their
spice and smokiness,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.120
Guajillo chilies for
their spice and sweetness,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.280
and Ancho chilies for their dark richness.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.780
We'll cover chilies with warm water

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.300
and let them soften
for about five minutes.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.300
Then we'll remove the
seeds and stems like this

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.480
or under running water
and set the peppers aside.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.490
But the spice doesn't stop there.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.660
Incorporating more spices
and seasonings is going

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.850
to give us a well-balanced birria

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.290
that's layered with flavor.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.860
Like chilies, everyone has their
go-to seasoning for birria.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.107
Cinnamon, coriander
seed, bay leaves, cloves,

00:02:23.107 --> 00:02:25.440
allspice, dried avocado leaves,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.220
black peppercorns, cumin,

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.600
and Mexican oregano.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.360
There are two types of oregano,

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.840
Italian and Mexican.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.570
While they share the same name,

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.740
they have different tastes and aromas.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:39.480
Italian or Mediterranean
oregano has minty undertones

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.080
while Mexican oregano has more
citrusy, earthy undertones.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.720
For our recipe we are are using cinnamon,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.960
coriander seed, black peppercorns,

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.760
allspice, and cloves.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.300
We'll add them to a medium skillet

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.320
and toast them over medium heat.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.830
Then we'll transfer them
to a spice grinder along

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.240
with our dried bay leaves
and Mexican oregano.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.320
Toasting and grinding our spices

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.630
will give our spice mix a
fresher more vibrant flavor.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:07.440
You want to grind them into
a fine powder like this.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.480
Now it's time to cook our meat.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.690
Traditionally the meat
is cooked underground.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.480
We found the best way to replicate

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.610
that method was using a Dutch oven

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.050
with a tight fitted lid.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:21.670
We'll remove our meat from the
refrigerator and pat it dry.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.180
This will absorb any excess moisture

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.660
that could keep our meat
from getting an even crust.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.470
Next we'll add a quarter cup of canola oil

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.800
to our pot over medium high heat.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.200
Once the oil is shimmery,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.700
add the meat and sear it on all sides.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.570
We want to create this
really great crust all over,

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.540
also known as the Maillard reaction.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.774
This process is going to
lock in yet another layer

00:03:44.774 --> 00:03:47.100
of flavor to our braising liquid.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.494
Once the meat is golden brown,

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:50.990
remove it from the pot and set it aside.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:53.410
We're going to go ahead
and add our yellow onions

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.260
and quartered Roma tomatoes.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.420
Sprinkle them with a tablespoon of salt

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:59.450
and let everything cook until the onions

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:01.020
and tomatoes begin to sweat,

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.810
about five to seven minutes.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.860
Then we'll add our garlic
and cook until fragrant.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:08.290
As if our kitchen
couldn't smell any better,

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.710
we're going to add the
spices we prepared earlier.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.150
Cook until they're aromatic,

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:14.700
about two minutes.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.780
Next, we'll add a little
apple cider vinegar

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:19.910
and eight cups of water.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.640
Why water and not beef or chicken broth?

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.670
We're actually building our
own broth right in this pot,

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:28.840
so adding more beef or
chicken broth isn't necessary.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.120
We'll return our seared meat to the pot,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.290
toss in those chilies,

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.144
and bring everything to a boil.

00:04:34.144 --> 00:04:37.080
Then we'll reduce our stew to a simmer

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.393
and cover it with a lid.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.070
Okay, it's been about 30 minutes.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:43.540
Let's give this a look.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.300
See how our mixture has reduced

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.790
and the chilies have completely softened?

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.300
That's our cue that it's time

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.620
to remove our chilies and blend them.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.010
We'll place them in a
high powered blender along

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.743
with two cups of our broth and blend.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.383
Smooth and silky.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.950
We'll add the chile
mixture back into our pot

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:07.143
and give it a stir.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.423
Now we'll start topping our
stew with banana leaves.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:18.800
The banana leaves are going
to add yet another layer

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.200
of flavor while also preventing
our meat from drying out.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.033
Quick tip.

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:24.850
If you can't find banana leaves,

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.770
use aluminum foil.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.020
And now for the hard part, waiting.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:32.150
We're going to cover pot
and transfer it to the oven

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.730
to cook for 90 minutes.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.500
It might be the longest
90 minutes of your life,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.700
but hang in there.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.430
See, that wasn't so bad.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:43.223
We'll take off the lid and
remove the banana leaves.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.310
Then remove the cooked meat from the pot,

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.990
place it in a large mixing bowl,

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.400
and start shredding.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.920
The meat should easily pull away

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.300
from the short rib bones like this.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.530
Now, it wouldn't be tacos
without tortillas and hey,

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:01.050
if you want to go the extra
mile and make your own,

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.260
more power to ya.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:05.590
But store bought tortillas
are just as good for this

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:07.590
and when it comes to birria tacos,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.200
our tortillas of choice are corn.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.980
Here's when the magic happens.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:15.300
Remember that warm flavorful
liquid left in our pot

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.840
after we removed the meat?

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.400
That's our consome.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.780
See that thin layer of
fat sitting at the top?

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.720
We're gonna dip the tortillas in that

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:26.790
and lay them flat in our
lightly oiled skillet.

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:30.190
Pan frying our tortillas
in the fat is what's going

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:33.320
to give our tacos that
perfectly crisp crust.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.630
We're crisping our tortilla on both sides

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:37.290
so our tacos don't fall apart.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:40.220
There's some debate about
what we're about to do next.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.320
We're going to top our
tortilla with cheese.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.420
We're using shredded Oaxaca cheese here

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.990
because it's the best for melting,

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:49.100
but mozzarella cheese
is also a good option.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:50.760
Then we'll add our shredded beef

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:52.680
and fold the tortilla in half,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.240
and we'll cook it until
the cheese begins to melt.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.823
Okay, time to flip.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:00.860
Look at that golden brown
color and crispy crust!

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.830
This is what we want.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:03.930
We'll continue cooking

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.110
to achieve the same on the other side.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:09.290
They say you eat with your
eyes and they're not wrong.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:10.400
Wow.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.840
We came, we cooked, and
now we're more than ready

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:17.300
to consome these birria tacos.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.480
All jokes aside,

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:21.400
we like to serve our birria
tacos with some cilantro,

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.160
pickled red onions, and lime.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.040
And don't forget the consome

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.150
because dunking in your taco in it makes

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.800
for one delicious bite.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:29.990
These birria tacos

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.550
are seriously worth every minute and mess,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.830
so do us a favor and make this recipe,

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:36.792
and dip it like it's hot.

00:07:36.792 --> 00:07:39.542
(relaxing music)

